8. Workspace Deletion Semantics

Dependent data (all without CASCADE)

1. area_version rows (FK → area.id)
2. area rows (FK → workspace.id)
3. workspace_member rows (FK → workspace.id)
4. In-memory presence state (ephemeral, no DB)

Proposed approach: explicit cascade in a @Transactional service method

DELETE FROM area_version WHERE area_id IN (SELECT id FROM area WHERE workspace_id = :id)
DELETE FROM area           WHERE workspace_id = :id
DELETE FROM workspace_member WHERE workspace_id = :id
DELETE FROM workspace      WHERE id = :id

All within one transaction. If the workspace doesn't exist, throw WorkspaceNotFoundException (404).

Justification: No new migration needed. The constraint order is deterministic. Native SQL via JdbcTemplate is the most direct approach (same pattern used in permanentDeleteArea).

Connected clients when their workspace is deleted

Before executing the physical delete, broadcast a WORKSPACE_DELETED WebSocket event to /topic/workspace/{id}. The frontend handles this event by navigating to /workspaces. This prevents the map user from being stuck on a 404-returning workspace without knowing why.

The WORKSPACE_DELETED event payload needs only the workspaceId (no area payload). A new event record or a separate broadcast call is sufficient.

After the broadcast, the physical delete proceeds. Subsequent REST calls by lingering clients return 404 — the frontend 404 handler on workspace-scoped calls also redirects to /workspaces as a fallback.

