16. Risks and Edge Cases

R1 — STOMP workspace-id header missing: If the frontend sends a CONNECT without workspace-id (e.g., an old client), PresenceService has no workspace context. Handling: log a warning, skip presence tracking for that session (don't crash). Presence gracefully degrades — the user is just not visible.

R2 — Concurrent workspace deletion: User A is on the map for workspace W while User B deletes W from the workspace list. User A's next REST call returns 404. The MapComponent must handle 404 on workspace-scoped calls gracefully (navigate to /workspaces). The WORKSPACE_DELETED WebSocket broadcast provides the proactive signal.

R3 — AreaService interface change ripple: AreaService's 8 public methods all gain a workspaceId parameter. All 12+ area-focused test classes call these methods indirectly via HTTP, not directly — so no test code changes. The AreaController already has workspaceId from @PathVariable — no change. The frontend AreaService needs all callers updated; map.ts is the only caller — this is a contained change.

R4 — Presence scoping regression in tests: PresenceIntegrationTest TC-PRESENCE-004/005 connect STOMP without workspace-id header (current code). Once the interceptor starts reading the header, these tests will have null workspaceId → silent presence skip → events won't fire on the workspace topic → tests may fail. The tests must be updated to send workspace-id in the STOMP CONNECT headers.

R5 — PROTOTYPE_WORKSPACE_ID env var removal: Production deployment environments (CI/CD, docker-compose.yml) that set PROTOTYPE_WORKSPACE_ID must remove it. If left set, it's harmless (unused), but documentation should note the change.

R6 — PrototypeProperties scope: Moving @EnableConfigurationProperties(PrototypeProperties.class) from SecurityConfig to DevConfig means it's only active in the dev profile. Tests use TestConfig which explicitly enables it, so tests are unaffected. Production context no longer has this bean — AuthService (which no longer uses it) and all production code are clean.

R7 — Performance seed workspace: The performance workspace (e0000000-…-0001) is inserted directly via SQL, bypassing the API. It will coexist with the new workspace management flow. No conflict.

R8 — Dev user without initial workspace: After DevDataInitializer stops seeding a workspace, the dev user starts with zero workspaces. DevTokenController still works. The first dev session must create a workspace manually or via the UI. This is the intended behavior.

